@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;motion] gallery-pinches-change-tile-size
A two-finger pinch on the mosaic MUST continuously change tile size through multiple
density levels and reverse while the fingers remain down. Density stops
MUST offer distinct sizes for uniform portrait, square and landscape cards, with one
to four columns at their stops, one column being full screen where
gallery-reels-at-one-column applies. Text MUST retain its size and wrapping when
releasing at a stop. At each stop every tile with words MUST show its words at that
stop's size (gallery-text-is-one-size): it is laid out in the shape of its cell, in a
frame its cell's size divided by how much larger than 13 sp its words show there, and
drawn scaled to its cell, so a big tile's words are the size of its neighbours'. It
MUST NOT rewrap, change its inset, or show or hide its labels under the fingers: it
keeps its layouts at the stops on either side, each drawn scaled to where the card is,
and the two cross-fade with its progress. The layout at the other stop is composed
ahead, while the gallery rests, and kept while the card shows, so neither the fingers
coming down nor crossing a stop composes a burst of layouts in one frame (Tavi, 4 November
2026: "Same for pinching. It's laggy right now."; every tile's second layout was composed
in the frame the fingers landed)
(Tavi's pick of 07 Prints, with its Gentle sizes, on the card looks canvas, 1 November
2026, replacing version 13 of the design canvas he picked on 28 October 2026, where
every tile was laid out once at three columns and drawn scaled with its cell).
Adjacent cached grids MUST remain coherent and nonoverlapping within each projection, sharing one focal card's fractional X/Y point and physical
size. Cards MUST move with the fingers from their first movement: no stop holds them
still and no glide starts at a threshold. One stop takes about a 30% pinch at every
size. Between two stops the cards follow 60% of the finger travel and, once the
fingers pass halfway, ease the rest in a 130 ms hop. Each card moves from its place in
the grid of the stop the gesture started on to its place in the other grid; lower
lines start later, each card waiting up to half the step by its reading order among
the cards either grid shows, except the focal card, which MUST NOT wait. A card that
changes line MUST slide out past one edge of its old line and in from the other edge
of its new one, shrinking by up to a tenth and fading from one drawing to the other.
Its top decides the line: a card that keeps its top but changes shape, such as two
squares high in one grid and one in the other, slides, and the focal card never
changes line. Behind the cards both grids MUST be drawn
whole and blurred, the old one fading out, so no blank paper shows between moving
cards. The fill MUST fade in over the first fifth of a step and out over the last, so
it never appears or vanishes in one frame. Only a
card's own tile exposes semantics or hit targets: its copy while it changes line, its
layout in the other grid and the blurred fill expose none, and the focal card is
drawn once. Reduced motion skips the hop. A new single-finger press MUST retain its
owning projection and focal node through tap or hold, and stops a landing where it
is until the press ends. Passing drag slop releases focal anchoring; a second finger
rebases a new pinch.
Vertical anchoring respects scroll bounds. The focal point stays where the fingers
met: moving both fingers together MUST NOT pan the gallery. At every moment of a
gesture the grids MUST cover the full width, clipping their outer edges rather than
leaving horizontal slack; where moving cards leave a gap, the blurred fill shows. The
profile and highlights keep their own scale. Release MUST land the way the fingers
were moving once they are a quarter of the way to the next stop, settling the same
geometry without an independent row animation, in 250 ms for a whole stop (the pace of
a Larger or Smaller step) and 250 ms times the square root of a shorter way, so a short
way is quick but never snaps. The landing MUST start at the speed the cards had as
the fingers let go, from still when they were held, and come to rest without
overshooting; the frame that shows it already moves. Reduced motion settles
synchronously. The gesture, its
cancellation and a remaining one-finger tail MUST NOT open a card, open its action
sheet, or trigger pull to refresh. Rendering MUST retain stable card keys and a lazy,
bounded visible window, without scanning all cards or changing image decode sizes
on each pointer frame. Starting, moving or letting go of a pinch MUST NOT rebuild any
card's tile: the cards move, and their copies and the blurred fill draw what the tiles
last drew. Pictures change their decode size only once the gallery is at rest, after the
landing's last frame (Tavi, 30 Oct 2026: "There are some frames that skip and at the end
it kind of snaps weirdly"; every tile rebuilt as the fingers came down and again as they
let go, and every picture reloaded at its new size on the landing's first frame). A
picture keeps the largest size it has been decoded at, so landing on more, smaller cards
rebuilds and reloads nothing. Ending a landing MUST draw every frame, even when the new size
has fewer rows than the list still holds, and MUST leave every card where the
landing's last frame drew it: the list takes the new size's rows and its scroll
position in the same pass. Equivalent larger/smaller actions MUST be accessible, and
each tab MUST retain its density and scroll position. Removing the gallery during
a gesture or settle MUST synchronously save a canonical density, the stop a landing
was headed for, and an anchored scroll request, so reusing its state cannot restore a fractional transition.
Source: Tavi, 22 October 2026, explicit pinch-to-enlarge/reduce requirement; the
recorded review of the same day, with Google Photos as the reference ("the
intermediate state where there is emptiness", "I shouldn't be able to pan"); the
recorded review of 23 October 2026 ("then they reorganize completely, which is weird
and doesn't feel good"); Tavi on the squares canvases, 28 October 2026 ("the cards
only move after a while of you pinching ... instead of moving while you pinch, which
is actually what I want", "All of them have transitions that last too long", "I don't
want to see blank spots between them"), who then picked O3b of the "Calmer steps for
O" canvas; the web showcase's port of O3b found the focal card wrapping when it
changed shape, fixed the same day; Tavi on the same port, 28 October 2026 ("at the
end of the transition it all pops for a frame"): the fill vanished in one frame; Tavi,
28 October 2026 ("It's when I pinch and let go without fully getting to the next stage.
Then it pops, instead of being smooth"): the landing started at three times its average
speed, and the gallery jumped once it had landed. The pace and curve match the web
showcase's fix of the same day.

@cc [owner:OctavianTocan,label:product;motion;architecture] gallery-scrolls-without-hitches
Scrolling the gallery MUST NOT compose a burst of cards in one frame: the cards a screen
above and below what shows are composed ahead, one a frame, nearest first, so a card is
ready before it scrolls into view. Pinching MUST NOT compose a burst either: the cards the
grids a stop past either side show are composed ahead too, and at rest in reels the tiles of
the grids around and a stop past are readied, unplaced and hidden from touch and accessibility,
and the pages stay composed, so pinching out of reels composes none at once. While the cards move under the fingers or land, no card
or other-size layout is composed ahead: those are readied at rest, so a pinch's frames only
move cards. On the way into reels the full-screen pages around the
fingers are composed one a frame, before they fade in. Drawing a frame MUST NOT need an offscreen layer per
card: a tile's fading last line is its paper laid over the words, and the blurred fade
at the foot of the gallery blurs each of its layers only as far as that layer reaches. While
the cards scroll or move under the fingers, the foot fade MUST NOT blur, only fade: each
blur layer draws the whole gallery again, and Tavi's 4 November recording of the
phone's frame bars showed drawing, not the app's own work, made frames late.
Source: Tavi, 4 November 2026: "We gotta optimize the app for scrolling to be super
smooth with no hitches or pops or lag of any sort." and "Same for pinching. It's laggy right now."

